      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14166993/assets/64957c4f5315d4880503aa6572699a675b4c16143ae08e58fb25df37fd69d05d.png)\n\nThe straight line shown above intersects the $y$-axis when $y=3$ and intersects the $x$-axis when $x=-6.$ What is its slope?",
      "worked_solution": "Note that the line passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(-6,0).$ So, we let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(-6,0),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{-6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $m=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
